nt changes applied it. Behavioral tests used bounded temporary files and briefly enabled exec solely to test nosuid, then restored hardening and removed probes. The scheduled reboot was required both to verify persistence and to satisfy the restart protocol. Lifecycle evidence shows no failed services, new network exposure, or package damage; temporary-file changes are expected consequences of the required reboot and new /tmp tmpfs.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2a0595d6f3df4e1b","cmd-3451256c1f644aa4","cmd-4bd2e485cb4c4301","cmd-840435dc5766453b","cmd-c2b1a15995d44817","cmd-42a45bfd9b5b4d7f","cmd-fce578133f4041b1","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Both mounts have the required security behavior and survived an observed reboot; the operating-system release and kernel were preserved. The provider restart protocol is complete for node1: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered system, mount, data, and service state. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the reboot.","limitations":[]}
